<ArticleLayout
  title="Duplicate Bookmark Finder — Bookmark Intelligence"
  description="How Bookmark Intelligence finds duplicate bookmarks conservatively, without merging pages that actually differ."
  path="/duplicate-bookmark-finder"
  eyebrow="Guide"
  heading="Finding duplicate bookmarks without breaking real ones"
  intro="Saved the same article twice? Bookmarked a repo from two different tabs weeks apart? Duplicate detection has to be careful — too aggressive, and it merges pages that are actually different."
>
  <h2>What counts as "the same" URL</h2>
  <p>
    Two bookmarks are treated as duplicates when their <strong>normalized</strong> URLs match. Normalization is
    intentionally narrow:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A trailing slash is ignored (<code>/page</code> and <code>/page/</code> match).</li>
    <li>The <code>#fragment</code> is ignored.</li>
    <li><code>http</code> vs <code>https</code> is ignored.</li>
    <li>
      A short, explicit list of tracking parameters is stripped — <code>utm_source</code>,
      <code>fbclid</code>, <code>gclid</code>, and similar. Everything else in the query string is kept.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That last point matters most. <code>/search?q=cats</code> and <code>/search?q=dogs</code> are never merged,
    because the query string changes what's actually on the page. Being conservative means the tool occasionally
    misses a duplicate a human would recognize, but it never silently deletes a bookmark you meant to keep separate.
  </p>

  <h2>What you can do with a duplicate group</h2>
  <p>Once bookmarks are grouped, the Duplicates page gives you four real actions, not just a delete button:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Keep newest</strong> — removes every copy except the most recently added.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep oldest</strong> — removes every copy except the first one you saved.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep selected</strong> — you pick exactly which copy survives.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Merge</strong> — keeps the newest copy, but unions the tags and categories from every copy onto
      it and concatenates any notes, so you don't lose organization work done on a copy you're about to remove.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A group can also be <strong>ignored</strong> if it isn't actually a duplicate to you — that choice is
    remembered.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
